WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.900
If every division keeps some daughters and spends others, how many neurons does the

2
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:08.900
cortex get?

3
00:00:09.583 --> 00:00:11.023
Start with a simple model.

4
00:00:11.823 --> 00:00:15.923
A pool of dividing cells, each dividing once per round, with none dying.

5
00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:18.163
Every cell makes two daughters.

6
00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:22.237
Call P the fraction of daughters that stay progenitors.

7
00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:25.037
Call Q the fraction that quit as neurons.

8
00:00:26.097 --> 00:00:27.117
Together they make one.

9
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.420
The next pool comes from the daughters that stay.

10
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.300
The neurons come from the daughters that quit.

11
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.240
This pool is every dividing cell, not just stem cells.

12
00:00:38.183 --> 00:00:41.523
Suppose Q is 0.5. Half the daughters quit.

13
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:44.203
Does the pool shrink, hold or grow?

14
00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:46.023
And does it make neurons?

15
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:54.127
It holds, and it makes neurons.

16
00:00:55.127 --> 00:00:58.507
It is as if every division keeps one daughter and spends one.

17
00:00:59.307 --> 00:01:01.227
A steady pool is not an idle one.

18
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.930
Below 0.5, the pool grows.

19
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.010
Above it, the pool shrinks.

20
00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:12.570
In mouse dorsal cortex, Takahashi and colleagues estimated about eleven cycles of

21
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.570
neurogenesis.

22
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.550
Q starts near zero.

23
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:20.530
It passes 0.5 in the eighth cycle, and reaches about 0.8 by the tenth.

24
00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:26.750
Takahashi measured Q for the ventricular zone proper, apart from cells dividing

25
00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.970
farther out. Those old compartments are not today's cell classes.

26
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.020
Follow one founder through that schedule.

27
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.200
The numbers are illustrative.

28
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.790
Early on, almost nothing is made, and the pool multiplies.

29
00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:43.153
Late, the pool is spent.

30
00:01:44.053 --> 00:01:49.193
Output peaks at cycle 9, just after Q passes 0.5, while the pool is still near its

31
00:01:49.193 --> 00:01:50.193
largest.

32
00:01:50.617 --> 00:01:51.957
The schedule is a lever.

33
00:01:52.897 --> 00:01:57.757
In this model, moving the crossing one cycle later nearly doubles the total, over

34
00:01:57.757 --> 00:01:58.977
the same eleven cycles.

35
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:05.067
That is why a few extra proliferative cycles are a candidate for how bigger cortices

36
00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:06.067
are built.

37
00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:12.807
Now a trap. Suppose every radial glial division makes a radial glial cell and an

38
00:02:12.807 --> 00:02:13.987
intermediate progenitor.

39
00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:17.107
In that round, no daughter quits.

40
00:02:17.847 --> 00:02:19.827
Counted as one pool, it seems to double.

41
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.610
Follow one radial glial cell for four cycles.

42
00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.590
Each intermediate progenitor divides into two neurons.

43
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.730
How many radial glia, and how many neurons?

44
00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:39.973
Still one radial glial cell, and six neurons.

45
00:02:40.913 --> 00:02:44.033
One more intermediate progenitor is about to make two more.

46
00:02:44.793 --> 00:02:47.253
That first doubling was not a gain in stem cells.

47
00:02:48.266 --> 00:02:49.486
So count two pools.

48
00:02:50.466 --> 00:02:54.446
In this model, radial glia are the source that keeps neurogenesis going.

49
00:02:55.466 --> 00:02:59.906
Intermediate progenitors multiply, and delay, what each spent daughter yields.

50
00:03:00.966 --> 00:03:02.326
The cycle itself slows.

51
00:03:03.366 --> 00:03:07.526
In mouse dorsal cortex, it lengthens from about 8 hours to about 18.

52
00:03:08.606 --> 00:03:10.546
Nearly all the added time is in G1.

53
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:16.450
One hypothesis: a longer G1 gives fate determinants more time to act.

54
00:03:17.383 --> 00:03:22.823
In cultured mouse embryos, a drug that lengthened G1 led to early neurogenesis.

55
00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:26.663
Whether a longer G1 causes the switch is still open.

56
00:03:27.666 --> 00:03:30.986
The first puzzle has an answer: the scaffold is the source.

57
00:03:31.866 --> 00:03:36.906
The second has a partial answer: the trip to the ventricle fits the centrosome and

58
00:03:36.906 --> 00:03:38.586
may let more cells share the surface.

59
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.800
What the cortex gets is a count: how many radial glia each division keeps, and what

60
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:45.920
each spent daughter yields.
